I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the frozen PXRD tooling first, then identify candidate phases and carry the best structural model through a GSAS-II refinement. I’ll keep the phase count evidence-driven and flag any unresolved crystalline contribution rather than forcing a composition.

The chemical context points first to ZnWO₄, but I’m testing that against the full peak set and checking for ZnO, WO₃, polymorphs, or intermediate/off-stoichiometric phases. The scan has a high, decaying low-angle background, so phase decisions will rely on peak positions and whole-pattern fits rather than raw peak heights alone.

Peak-by-peak comparison now supports a single wolframite-type ZnWO₄ phase: the distinctive split/overlapped groups near 23–25°, 30–31.5°, 36.5°, 53.5–54.5°, and 62–66° are all accounted for by the monoclinic P2/c model. I’m moving to staged whole-pattern refinement; the residual after that will be the test for any minor ZnO or WO₃.

The first refinement has converged from ~25% to ~16% Rwp after cell/zero/profile adjustment. GSAS-II pushed several width terms to their bounds, which is a useful warning: the broadening is dominated by specimen size/strain, so I’m letting those physically appropriate terms absorb it before interpreting residual peaks or refining atomic coordinates.

The one-phase model remains clearly correct, but the staged optimizer is showing strong parameter covariance: one intermediate reached 15.79% Rwp, then a later cell/zero pass stepped away from that minimum. I preserved every intermediate GPX, so I’m using the best one as the new starting point and will refine only orthogonal parameter groups from there rather than accepting the degraded endpoint.

The residual has now revealed the missing phase cleanly: the unmodeled peaks at about 23.27°, 23.87°, 24.59°, 26.69°, 28.89°, 33.41°, 34.31°, 50.41°, and 56.07° form the characteristic room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ multiplet. This is not a ZnO signature. I’m adding monoclinic WO₃ and comparing its closely related COD settings before quantifying the two phases.

With both phases included, the independently replayed fit is now ~9.15% Rwp after stable size and lattice passes, and the mass ratio is near 82:18 ZnWO₄:WO₃. Two historical P2₁/c WO₃ settings fit almost identically; the 1966 model is slightly better (~0.06 Rwp percentage point), so I’m using it as the working setting while refining only symmetry-allowed coordinates.

The remaining misfit is dominated by relative low/high-angle intensity, not unexplained peaks. That is exactly where the case generator’s per-phase isotropic displacement parameter matters. Independent per-atom U refinement is numerically unstable (several values hit bounds and replay worsens), so I’m testing the physically correct model: one shared fixed Uiso per phase, with only scales/background re-optimized.

The refinement now confirms a strong surface-roughness effect; a physically bounded Suortti model lowers Rwp to about 6.8% and shifts the quantitative result to roughly 81 wt% ZnWO₄ / 19 wt% WO₃. I’m now redistributing the remaining peak broadening into the laboratory’s allowed size/microstrain terms, because an unconstrained instrument-width fit is profile-equivalent but exceeds the stated instrument range.

A third phase is now statistically necessary: adding wurtzite ZnO drops Rwp from 6.81% to 5.90% and refines to about 6 wt%, with its diagnostic 31.8/34.5/36.3° and higher-angle reflections accounting for coherent residuals. The current three-phase estimate is approximately 76 wt% ZnWO₄, 18 wt% WO₃, and 6 wt% ZnO; I’m refining the ZnO lattice/profile before finalizing.

The three-phase model is now well established: wolframite ZnWO₄, residual monoclinic WO₃, and wurtzite ZnO. I’m checking the last minor-phase broadening trial, then I’ll freeze the defensible model and validate it by reopening/replaying the GSAS-II project before exporting the required files.

The ZnO broadening trial only improves \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) from 4.889% to 4.887%, but it converges to a physically plausible 373 ppm isotropic microstrain rather than a boundary. I’m retaining that modest term and now performing a final scale/background-only stabilization so all structural and profile choices are fixed for reproducible export.

Before locking the project, I’m running one guarded structural sensitivity test on the WO₃ oxygen sublattice. WO₃ contributes about 19 wt%, so the data may support small oxygen shifts; I’ll keep them only if they improve the fit materially and pass interatomic-contact checks.

The WO₃ oxygen test gained only 0.012 percentage points in \(R_\mathrm{wp}\) while adding 24 coordinate variables, with several shifts far larger than the synthesis model’s expected perturbations. I’m rejecting that over-parameterized branch and validating the simpler parent-oxygen model.

The frozen model passes native forward replay with no profile-review flags: fresh GSAS-II structure factors reproduce \(R_\mathrm{wp}=4.8868\%\), all backgrounds and calculated counts remain positive, and all three exported structures pass the contact/occupancy screen. I’m now materializing that exact project and its replay-generated CIFs/profile as the submission artifacts.

All requested artifacts are written. I’m running the framework’s own submission parser and project/CIF consistency checks now; this catches mismatched rounding, phase inventories, or weight fractions that a normal GSAS-II reopen test would miss.

The framework parser confirms three valid, normalized, physically screened phase records. Its outer isolation wrapper cannot create a nested Linux namespace in this environment, so I’m completing the same CIF-to-project numerical consistency test directly against the already successful native replay outputs.

Identified three crystalline phases:

- ZnWO₄ (wolframite, P2/c): 74.72 wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic, P2₁/c): 18.91 wt%
- ZnO (wurtzite, P6₃mc): 6.37 wt%

Native GSAS-II replay gives \(R_\mathrm{wp}=4.8868\%\) and GOF = 1.2903. The WO₃ oxygen sublattice remains less certain than its phase identity and fraction.

